WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.625
JENNY BROWN: We've
covered upload speed

00:00:01.625 --> 00:00:02.910
hiccups for large files.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.400
But what about when
it's time to read?

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.350
On this episode of
Cloud Storage Bytes,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.090
you'll need your
reading glasses,

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.125
because we're talking
large file downloads.

00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:13.915
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.460
We've all been there, staring
at our watch wondering

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.400
how much longer it's going
to take for that large file

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.890
to move from cloud storage
to the virtual machine

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.170
where computation occurs.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.470
Even if it's only two
minutes, that's an eternity

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.140
as far as we're concerned,
especially when we're

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.340
paying for that computing
power during our download.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.030
Ouch.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.440
When looking at
our options here,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.470
we might recall that gsutil
saved the day for our uploads.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.320
But that doesn't seem to be
the case for our downloads.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.800
We even found that curl
outperforms gsutil here.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.210
But why?

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.300
And just when we thought
we had it all figured out,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.070
luckily, I'm on the case.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.770
And as it turns out, the
default settings for gsutil

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.070
don't work particularly well
with large file downloads.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.810
This is because these
default settings

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:05.170
are designed to spread downloads
across four threads using

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.760
one process.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.830
We should be able to
improve the performance

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.480
by limiting the
number of threads

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.290
and forcing the system to use
multiple processes instead.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.070
For a quick recap, processes
are instances of execution,

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.530
while threads are
individual executions that

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.900
run inside of a process.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:27.380
We want more processes, but
each focused on only one task.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.130
So first up, limiting
the number of threads.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.230
To do this, there is
a command line option

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.970
to specify the thread count.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.190
We'll go ahead and
reduce it to one.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.680
With this small change, we've
improved the performance.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.260
But we're still not at the
level that curl is showing when

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.360
we're fetching through HTTP.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.900
So next up, forcing
multiple processes.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.000
To do this, we're going
to have a throwback moment

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.770
to a previous
Cloud Storage Bytes

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.530
where we discussed a
parallel composite upload.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.860
Now, it's time for parallel
or sliced downloads, which

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.170
we can trigger by
telling gsutil to use

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.190
a certain number of components.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.820
So to recap-- decreasing
the number of threads

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.620
and increasing the
number of components

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.840
can work together to
dramatically improve

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.130
your download speed.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.870
There's plenty more to come
about optimizing performance,

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.000
but that's for another episode.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.480
If you want to know more
about downloading large files,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.410
check out the links
in the description.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:21.922
And as always, don't forget
to subscribe, give us alike,

00:02:21.922 --> 00:02:24.130
and tell us what you want
to learn about Google Cloud

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.000
Storage.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.760
Thanks for joining us for this
quick bite of cloud storage.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.570
See you next time.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.920
